From 2cb5e31be3aacfcf4e2b2b98e486a8123adc5d95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:47:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 74/74] HPF: the HP ladder saturates as in the original
 firmware again, so its self-oscillation no longer drowns out the music

A user on v16 heard a painful high tone with the song's HPF on with resonance while turning the song's LPF. From about
70 % resonance the HP ladder oscillates on its own, a sine at its cutoff (2.2 kHz with the knob at 30), whatever comes
in, at about the level where its tanh limits. The LPF after it (H2L, the default route) hides it while the LPF is below
it and lets it through as the LPF opens, so it seems to come from turning the LPF.

That level is too high since community #336 (04faf186, "Morphable and Driveable and Parallel filters"):
- original firmware (fc76a9cd, Synthstrom's code): voices dsp/filter/filter_set.cpp:58 HPF_LONG_SATURATION 3, used in
  renderHPFLong() at :108/:111/:115; the song's, a kit's and an audio track's filters
  model/global_effectable/global_effectable.cpp:366-367 renderHPF(..., 2), i.e. filter_set.cpp:42/:47 "2 +
  extraSaturation" = 4 (community #304 still had 3 / 4)
- 1.2.1 to v16: dsp/filter/hpladder.cpp:101 getTanHAntialiased(a, ..., 1), :106 getTanHUnknown(a, 2), :104 the working
  value with << 2, for every context. Community main (47b1d92b, 2026-09-25) still has these values, same lines.
So the ladder limits 2 to 3 bits higher: its whistle is 12 dB (voices) to 18 dB (song, kit, track) louder than in the
original firmware. In the real firmware in the emulator (tests/song's song, song view, HPF knob 30, resonance 40): the
whistle at -7.3 dBFS against the music's -33.6 (peaks at 2.3 x full scale, clipped); now -24.5 dBFS, peaks 0.39.

Now FilterSet::renderLong() / renderLongStereo() take the HP ladder's saturation amount, HpLadderFilter::
kSaturationVoice (3, the default: Voice) or kSaturationGlobal (4: GlobalEffectable::processFilters(), the song, kits,
audio tracks), and the ladder's loops use it as a template constant (no cost per sample; +4.8 kB of code). The
antialiased tanh's last working value is written with the same shift as that amount (1.2.1: << 2 with an amount of 1).
The amount sits in the padding after an unused bool, so a voice's FilterSet stays the same size.

What changes: only the HP ladder above ~39 % resonance (hpfProcessedResonance > 750000000; the antialiased tanh from
~50 %). Below that, bit-exact; the SVF HPF modes and all LPFs unchanged. Above it the ladder limits earlier: loud
peaks are softened a little sooner, and the self-oscillation is as loud as in the original firmware (the song's
whistle ~17 dB quieter, a voice's ~12 dB), no longer far above the music.

Tests (delivery repo, tests/filters/run.sh, ARM=1 on the Cortex-A9 in the emulator; in run_all.sh): the song's, a
kit's, a synth's and a kit row's filters, every HPF mode, route and LPF mode, resonance 25-50, the LPF turned in
0.25 s and 2 s: v16 fails (HP ladder up to +12.7 dB against the music on the song's filters, +16.1 synth, +16.2 kit
row), this passes (HP ladder at most +4.3 dB, SVF +5.5 as in v16). Turning the LPF adds nothing above 8 kHz in either.
Filter bench (tests/bench/filters, ARM): every case identical to v16 except "HP ladder res60" (mono, stereo) and the
random cases (they include high HPF resonance). Song test (EMU_OPTS="--init-sounds --seed 1" tests/song/run.sh):
demand 87a7df292189e341, digital 4473b3155dcfa0e8, unchanged (its HPFs have no resonance).

